# Why vote when your vote almost never matters?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "The paradox of voting Duty, expressive voting, and what people actually do"
next: {"title":"Is \"rational choice\" a theory or a tautology?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/97-is-rational-choice-a-theory-or-a.cast"}

## How often does one vote decide?
sources:
    - id: uselect2020
      title: 2020 November General Election Turnout Rates
      authors: US Elections Project (McDonald)
      year: 2020
      finding: About 158 million ballots were counted; turnout of the voting-eligible population was about two in three, the highest since 1900.
    - id: mh2003
      title: The Empirical Frequency of a Pivotal Vote
      authors: Casey B. Mulligan and Charles G. Hunter
      year: 2003
      finding: 'NBER WP 8590 (2001); Public Choice 116 (2003). Table 1: 16,577 US House elections, 1898–1992. One was decided by a single vote: New York''s 36th district in 1910, 20,685 to 20,684; a further recount found 20,690 to 20,684 and both parties recognised it was not that close (Section IV). One vote in 100,000 in US elections ''mattered''.'
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w8590
    - id: downs1957
      title: An Economic Theory of Democracy
      authors: Anthony Downs
      year: 1957
      finding: A voter who weighs the costs of voting against the expected benefit of affecting the outcome will rationally abstain in large elections.
    - id: pr1985
      title: Voter participation and strategic uncertainty
      authors: Palfrey and Rosenthal
      year: 1985
      finding: In game-theoretic turnout models with uncertainty about others, equilibrium turnout in large electorates is close to zero for voters with positive net costs.
    - id: gse2012
      title: What is the probability your vote will make a difference?
      authors: Gelman, Silver and Edlin
      year: 2012
      doi: 10.1111/j.1465-7295.2010.00272.x
      finding: A week before the 2008 US presidential election, the average voter's chance of being decisive was about 1 in 60 million; about 1 in 10 million in New Mexico, Virginia, New Hampshire and Colorado.

    text big "158 million" #2f6b8f cites ["uselect2020"] x 420 y 585 font_size 84 hidden true
    text bigsub "votes · US 2020" #3d3833 x 420 y 515 font_size 32 hidden true
    icon box size 130 set twemoji x 150 y 560 or ["ballot box","ballot"] icon_look picture of "ballot box with ballot" hidden true
    text share "2 in 3 eligible · most since 1900" #8f887c cites ["uselect2020"] x 420 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    text cost "cost: an hour, a trip, an evening" #b5482e x 420 y 425 font_size 28 hidden true
    scale races cites ["mh2003"] x 70 y 250 width 860 font_size 26 label "House races won by one vote, of 16,577" min 1 max 10000 value 1 log true hidden true
    scratch house cites ["mh2003"] work ["New York, 1910","20,685 to 20,684","recount: 20,690 to 20,684"] x 790 y 520 font_size 26 hidden true
    text callback "1 in 60 million\n→ rational ignorance\n→ stay home (Downs)" #b5482e cites ["gse2012","downs1957"] x 840 y 540 font_size 26 hidden true
    text loop "nobody votes\n→ you decide?" #8a5fa8 cites ["pr1985"] x 800 y 520 font_size 32 hidden true
    text anyway "voted anyway" #2f6b8f x 790 y 585 font_size 40 hidden true
    mark loopx around loop kind strike hidden true

    card title "How often does one vote decide?"

Why vote, when your one vote almost never decides anything?
    draw box

In 20 (@draw big bigsub@)20, about 158 million Americans voted: two in three of tho (@draw share@)se eligible, the highest share since 1900.

B: And every one of them gave up something for it. An hour in (@draw cost@) a queue, the trip, the evening reading up on candidates.

    erase cost

So how often does one vote really decide? Casey Mulligan and  (@draw races@)Charles Hunter counted, across 16,577 US House races from 1898 to 1992. Guess how many came down to a single vote.

    ask "Click on the line where you think it is." on races store g relative true default "100"
        right: One race: close to your {g}.
        wrong: You said {g}: it was one race.

One. New York's thirt (@draw house_box house_line_1 house_line_2@)y-sixth district, in 1910: 20,685 votes to 20,684.
    highlight races

B: And even that one didn't hold: a recount found t (@draw house_line_3@)he winner six votes ahead, and both parties agreed it wasn't that close.

    erase house

It's the same arithmetic as the one in sixty (@draw callback@) million that makes voters rationally ignorant. By Downs's count, a calculating voter should stay home.

    erase callback

B: Hold on. If everyone reasoned like that, (@draw loop@) nobody would vote, and then my vote would decide everything.

True, and economists have chased that loop. But with millions of possible vo (@highlight big@)ters, even the game-theory ver (@draw loopx@)sions predict that almost everyone stays home.

    erase loop loopx

So the puzzle stands: on a cost-benefit count, voting looks like a bad  (@draw anyway@)deal, and two in three Americans did it anyway.

    pause 0.5

## The calculus of voting
vars:
    zeros:
      value: 7.77815125
      min: 1
      max: 9
      step: 0.01
      decimals: 2
    B:
      value: 10000
      min: 0
      max: 100000
      step: 500
    C:
      value: 20
      min: 0
      max: 100
      step: 1
    D:
      value: 0
      min: 0
      max: 100
      step: 1
    Nd:
      expr: 10^zeros
    pB:
      expr: B/Nd
    R:
      expr: pB - C + D
    vote:
      expr: min(1,max(0,floor(R/1000000)+1))
sources:
    - id: ro1968
      title: A Theory of the Calculus of Voting
      authors: Riker and Ordeshook
      year: 1968
      finding: A citizen votes if R = pB − C + D > 0, where D is the satisfaction from voting itself (civic duty, affirming allegiance).
    - id: gse2012
      title: What is the probability your vote will make a difference?
      authors: Gelman, Silver and Edlin
      year: 2012
      doi: 10.1111/j.1465-7295.2010.00272.x
      finding: Average chance of a decisive vote in the 2008 US presidential election about 1 in 60 million.
    - id: egk2007
      title: 'Voting as a rational choice: why and how people vote to improve the well-being of others'
      authors: Edlin, Gelman and Kaplan
      year: 2007
      finding: If voters care about benefits to others, the social benefit grows with the electorate roughly as fast as the chance of being decisive falls, so voting can be rational for altruists.

    math formula cites ["ro1968"] tex "R = pB - C + D" size 40 colors.C #b5482e colors.D #5f8a4e colors.p #b8860b colors.R #2f6b8f x 840 y 600 hidden true
