    scratch alt cites ["egk2007"] work [{"tex":"B = N \\cdot b"},{"tex":"p \\approx k / N"},{"tex":"pB \\approx k \\cdot b"},"$100 × 330 M ÷ 60 M ≈ $550"] x 370 y 360 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "The calculus of voting"

B: Write the voter's sum down, as William Riker and Peter (@draw formula@) Ordeshook did in 1968: R equals p B, minus C, plus D.

B: p is the chan (@draw pread@)ce your vote decides, one in sixty million, and B is what your s (@draw bread illus@)ide's win is worth to you: say ten thousand dollars.

B: C is what  (@draw base nC barC vC@)voting costs you: the hour, the trip. Call it twenty dollars. And D st (@draw nD barD vD@)ays at zero for now.

p times B (@draw npB barpB vpB@) we already know: next to nothing. S (@draw nR barRp barRn vR@)o R is about minus twenty dollar (@draw stay vote@)s. Stay home.

The new question is how big B would have to be before voting paid for itself.
    highlight bread

    quiz "With p = 1 in 60 million and C = $20, how big must B be for voting to pay?" judge false voice a
        right: Divide the cost by the chance.
        * About $20,000
        * About $1 million
        * Over $1 billion

B: Twe (@draw need_box need_line_1@)nty dollars times (@draw need_line_2@) sixty million: one point two  (@draw need_line_3@)billion dollars.

So only someone with a billion riding on the result votes for the payoff.
    highlight need_line_3

    erase need illus

B: Riker and Ordeshook's way out w (@highlight formula part D@)as D: the satisfaction of doing your duty, of standing up to be counted.

Give D (@animate D 25 duration 2.5@) twenty-five dollars' worth, and the decision flips. Vote.

B: Try it yourself: drag p up to a small-town council race, or set D back to zero, and find where the decision flips.
    explore params ["zeros","B","C","D"]

Look at what really decides it, though. Almo (@highlight barD barC@)st never p times B: it's whether D beats C.

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own
        intro: There's a twist hiding in B.

@math

    erase mathq

    fade fig formula to 0.15

B: Aaron Edlin, Andrew Gelman and Noah Kaplan saw a t (@draw alt_box alt_line_1@)wist. Say you care a little about everyone: b dollars for each of N people.

In a close rac (@draw alt_line_2@)e, the chance of a tie shrinks roughly like one over N.

B: Multiply them, and N cancels: p ti (@draw alt_line_3@)mes B stays about the same, however big the country.

A hundred dollars for each of 330 milli (@draw alt_line_4@)on Americans, at one in sixty million: about five hundred and fifty dollars. That beats C.

    erase alt

@done

    erase mathq

    fade fig formula to 1

    pause 0.3

## Duty, pressure, and what people do
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: 'Three for three: your neighbours would be impressed.'
    good: 'Mostly right: the sum works when you know which term moved.'
    poor: Look for the term that changed, then the answer follows.
    none: Skipping the vote? Downs would understand.
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - No letter
      - Duty letter
      - Neighbours letter
    values:
      - 29.7
      - 31.5
      - 37.8
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: '%'
      decimals: 1
    bar_colors: same
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 45
    y_label: Turnout, Michigan 2006 (%)
    box:
      x: 60
      'y': 200
      w: 560
      h: 440
sources:
    - id: ggl2008
      title: 'Social Pressure and Voter Turnout: Evidence from a Large-Scale Field Experiment'
      authors: Gerber, Green and Larimer
      year: 2008
      finding: 'Before the August 2006 Michigan primary, about 180,000 households got one of four mailings or none: turnout 29.7% with no mailing, 31.5% with a civic-duty letter, 37.8% with a letter showing household and neighbours'' voting records.'
    - id: ghmr2020
      title: 'One in a Million: Field Experiments on Perceived Closeness of the Election and Voter Turnout'
      authors: Gerber, Hoffman, Morgan and Raymond
      year: 2020
      finding: Polls signalling a close race in the 2010 and 2014 US gubernatorial elections changed voters' beliefs about closeness but had no detectable effect on turnout.
    - id: gse2012
      title: What is the probability your vote will make a difference?
      authors: Gelman, Silver and Edlin
      year: 2012
      doi: 10.1111/j.1465-7295.2010.00272.x
      finding: Average chance of a decisive vote in the 2008 US presidential election about 1 in 60 million.

    text dq "What is D?" #5f8a4e x 810 y 600 font_size 40 hidden true
    text d1 "duty" #3d3833 x 810 y 530 font_size 34 hidden true
    text d2 "expression" #3d3833 x 810 y 480 font_size 34 hidden true
    text d3 "others' eyes" #3d3833 x 810 y 430 font_size 34 hidden true
    text plus8 "+8 points" #2f6b8f cites ["ggl2008"] x 810 y 560 font_size 44 hidden true
    text close "\"close race\" letters:\nturnout barely moves" #3d3833 cites ["ghmr2020"] x 810 y 440 font_size 30 hidden true
    math eq tex "R = pB - C + D" size 40 colors.D #5f8a4e x 810 y 600 hidden true
    text relabel "explanation\nor relabelling?" #8a5fa8 x 810 y 500 font_size 32 hidden true
    text s1 "one vote rarely decides:\nconsensus" #3d3833 cites ["gse2012"] x 810 y 470 font_size 28 hidden true
    text s2 "social pressure works:\nmostly agreed" #3d3833 cites ["ggl2008"] x 810 y 380 font_size 28 hidden true
    text s3 "is voting rational?\ncontested" #b5482e x 810 y 290 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Duty, pressure, and what people do"

If D carries the (@draw dq@) whole decision, it had better be something real. So what's in it?

B: Duty, (@draw d1@) for a start. Then expressio (@draw d2@)n, cheering for your side like a football fan. And the eyes  (@draw d3@)of other people.

Before a Michigan primary in 2006, A (@draw axes@)lan Gerber, Donald Green and Christopher Larimer sent letters to 180,000 households.

With no l (@draw bar_1@)etter, 29.7 percent voted. A letter urging  (@draw bar_2@)them to do their civic duty: 31.5.

B: The third letter listed whether you and your neighbours had voted, and promised an update after the election. Guess its turnout.
    highlight d3

    ask "Drag the third bar to the turnout you expect." on bar_3 store g default "31"
        right: 37.8 percent: close to your {g}.
        wrong: You said {g}: the neighbours letter got 37.8 percent.

    erase dq d1 d2 d3

37.8 percent. Eight poi (@draw plus8@)nts more, from one sheet of paper.

B: And the chance of being pivotal? Telling  (@draw close@)people their race was neck and neck, in field experiments in 2010 and 2014, barely moved turnout.

    erase plus8 close

B: But isn't  (@draw eq@)D just a name for whatever makes the sum come out right? P (@draw relabel@)eople vote, so D must be big.

Fair worry. D earns its place only if it predicts somet (@highlight bar_2 bar_3@)hing new, like the neighbours letter beating the duty letter. Whether every choice can be rescued this way is the next question.

    erase relabel

How sure are we? That o (@draw s1@)ne vote almost never decides a big election: consensus.

B: That  (@draw s2@)social pressure moves turnout: mostly agreed. Whether vot (@draw s3@)ing is rational at all: contested.

    quiz "Harbourtown's council election: 900 voters, and the last one was decided by 4 votes. Which term of R = pB − C + D differs most from a national election?" intro "Three new cases." voice b
        right: p: with a few hundred voters and a tight race, the chance of being pivotal is far larger, by tens of thousands of times.
        wrong: Few voters, a tiny margin: which term grows? Here, p.
        + p
        * C
        * D

    quiz "A country lets people vote from home on their phones. Which term falls?"
        right: C: no queue, no trip, so the cost of voting drops.
        wrong: Think of what the queue and the trip cost you: here, C.
        * p
        * B
        + C

    quiz "Going by the voting evidence, which letter most likely raises blood donations?" voice b
        right: The donor list: being seen did far more for turnout than a reminder of duty.
        wrong: Which letter moved turnout most? Here, the donor list.
        * Donating is your duty
        * Stocks are low this month
        + Neighbours will see the donor list

So why vote? Not for the one-in-sixty-million chance. For duty,  (@highlight bar_3@)for your side, and for the people who will know whether you did.

    pause 0.5

## Where next
